---
// src/components/kit/Hero.astro — the hero (spec §5 #2). Picked 2026-09-18: the split
// layout — copy left, photo right, trust chips under the lede, on the bone surface.
//
// THE PHOTO IS THE PAGE'S. The hero used to fall back to one of the kit's own puppy masters
// (src/assets/puppies/Cheryl1.jpeg) when a caller passed none — carried deliberately while the
// four pages built before working rule 16 mounted it that way (spec §9 amendment 10.7). All
// four now pass their own photograph at its original path (working rule 11), the board
// preview passes each record's, and the kit specimen names its own master, so the default is
// gone: a hero with no photo either says `media="none"` or stops the build, because a
// borrowed puppy on a page that never chose it is a claim, not a placeholder.
//
// SIZES. What `.pic` paints depends on the ARRANGEMENT and on the viewport band, and the list
// it declares is built per arrangement from measurements at twelve widths — see `PIC_SIZES`
// below, and `img-sizes-matches-box`, which measures the promise against the box on every
// built page. This header used to say "~560px on desktop", which was the split hero's column
// stated as the whole component's behaviour. `.pic` is the LCP element, so it is eager with
// fetchpriority="high" rather than lazy. The crop is CSS (aspect-ratio + object-fit), never
// a baked one, so one master serves every width.
//
// HEIGHT AND DOM ORDER (rules/design.md rule 10, spec §11 amendment 3c). The source site's
// hero puts the image first in the DOM and keeps the band short. Both hold here: the photo
// element PRECEDES the copy in source order and `order` puts it back on the right visually,
// and at >=1024px the band is held between 390 and 450px. Nothing is clipped to get there —
// the copy is SIZED to fit (3xl heading, two-line lede, tight block margins) and the PHOTO
// is what the ceiling crops. Below 1024px the height is auto, because a phone hero that
// clipped its own CTA would be worse than a tall one.
// scripts/measure_canvas_heights.mjs measures the overflow, the LEDE's own overflow and the
// CTA row's bottom edge at 1024, 1100 and 1280, and
// test_measured_hero_fits_its_clamp_without_clipping_anything fails on any of the three.
// The lede is measured separately because it is the one clamped element here: a clamp hides
// its overflow, so a third line disappears instead of pushing anything past the ceiling.
//
// HEADING LEVEL. `as` exists because a preview page can mount a hero under a page that
// already owns the only <h1>. On a real page the default 'h1' is correct and is what
// project 4 will use.
import type { HTMLAttributes } from 'astro/types';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { SITE } from '../../lib/site';
import Button from './Button.astro';
type Props = HTMLAttributes<'section'> & {
  as?: 'h1' | 'h2';
  /** The three copy props. ALL DEFAULT TO EMPTY, and an empty one renders no element at all.
   *  They used to default to a real eyebrow, a real headline and a real lede — "KC registered ·
   *  Carlisle", "Blue Staffy puppies raised in a family home", "Health-tested parents, Kennel
   *  Club paperwork" — which is the 2026-09-20 review's defect in its last hiding place: a
   *  component asserting a page's credentials for it. True on the homepage, a sales line on a
   *  privacy policy, and on neither of them a claim the page's own board record approved. */
  eyebrow?: string;
  title?: string;
  lede?: string;
  /** The photo. An `ImageMetadata` (a src/assets import) goes through astro:assets and is
   *  re-encoded and hashed; a STRING is a public path taken verbatim, which is how the
   *  legacy `/images/…` files are reused (working rule 11: every existing image keeps its
   *  original path). REQUIRED unless `media` is 'none' — there is no default photo. */
  image?: ImageMetadata | string;
  imageAlt?: string;
  /** Intrinsic size of a STRING `image`. astro:assets supplies both for an `ImageMetadata`
   *  import, but a public path is copied verbatim and nothing knows its dimensions — so an
   *  `<img>` with no `width`/`height` ships, the browser reserves no box for it, and the
   *  page reflows when it loads. `img_dims` (scripts/final_page_audit.py) is blocking on
   *  exactly that. Required in practice whenever `image` is a string; the board record
   *  carries the numbers in its `assets` row. */
  imageWidth?: number;
  imageHeight?: number;
  /** Candidate list for a STRING `image`, e.g. "/images/x-440.webp 440w, /images/x.webp 612w".
   *  astro:assets builds one for an `ImageMetadata` import; a public path has whatever
   *  siblings were baked beside it and nothing here can know them, so the page states them.
   *  Without it the `sizes` below has no candidates to choose between and the one file is
   *  decoded at whatever the slot happens to be — which is `img-srcset-within-2x` (blocking)
   *  the moment the hero card paints narrower than half the file's intrinsic width, as it
   *  does at 375px. Omitted renders no `srcset`, exactly as before. */
  imageSrcset?: string;
  /** The box the photo paints, for a page whose geometry is not `PageShell`'s.
   *
   *  `PIC_SIZES` below is measured on pages inside the shell, which reserves the dial its own
   *  196px column at 1024 and above — so the same split hero paints 408px on a real page and
   *  503px on `/kit-preview/`, which mounts the specimen outside the shell deliberately. A
   *  23% difference cannot be split between them by one list, and neither reading is wrong;
   *  they are different boxes. So a page outside the shell states its own, and every page
   *  inside it says nothing and gets the measured default. `img-sizes-matches-box` reads both
   *  against what they actually paint. */
  imageSizes?: string;
  /** The trust chips under the lede. DEFAULTS TO NONE (project 4, 2026-09-20 review). They
   *  used to be three hard-coded claims — "KC registered", "DNA-tested parents", "Raised in
   *  the home" — which is a component asserting a page's credentials for it: true on the
   *  homepage, a sales line on a privacy policy, and a claim the page's own board record
   *  never approved on any of them. A page that wants them passes them. */
  chips?: string[];
  /** The CTA row. DEFAULTS TO NONE, for the same reason and one more: the two buttons were
   *  hard-coded LINKS, so every page mounting a hero carried two hrefs its board record did
   *  not list and `scripts/link_parity_check.py` could not reconcile. A page passes the
   *  buttons its record's `links` and `cta` allow. */
  ctas?: { label: string; href: string; kind?: 'primary' | 'outline' | 'inverse' | 'text' }[];

  // ── working rule 16: the per-page arrangement ───────────────────────────────────────────
  // Five props, ALL OPTIONAL and none of them with a default that asserts content. `layout`,
  // `align` and `ledge` come from the picked StyleDef (src/lib/boardStyles.ts); `aside`,
  // `mosaic`, `stats` and `ticks` are the DATA a ledge or a layout needs, and a ledge whose
  // data the page did not supply renders nothing at all. That is the 2026-09-20 review's rule
  // restated: a hero states only what the page that mounted it handed it, so an about page's
  // quote band is not silently available to a privacy policy.
  /** The arrangement of copy against media. `split` is the shipped two-column hero. */
  layout?: 'split' | 'stacked' | 'mosaic' | 'panel' | 'bleed';
  /** Where the copy column's text sits. */
  align?: 'left' | 'center';
  /** Which side the photo is on, mirrored from the box's `bl-media-*` class so the component
   *  can key its own rules on it. Keying on `.bl-media-*` instead would put component rules
   *  in board-styles.css and lose the Astro scope hash that keeps them from tying with it. */
  media?: 'none' | 'left' | 'right' | 'top';
  /** Which block sits under the lede (or, for `aside`, beside the copy). */
  ledge?: 'none' | 'chips' | 'stats' | 'aside' | 'ticks';
  /** `ledge: 'ticks'` — a checked list of things the page can actually back. */
  ticks?: string[];
  /** `ledge: 'stats'` — figures the PAGE resolved from data/*.json. The hero never derives
   *  one: it prints what it is given, and a page with nothing to print passes none. */
  stats?: { n: string; label: string }[];
  // `ledge: 'aside'` is a SLOT, not a prop. A guide's aside is a fact table, an about page's
  // is somebody's sentence and a blog index's is a contents list, and a prop shaped to hold
  // all three is a prop that has stopped describing anything. The page passes the markup:
  //
  //     <Hero ledge="aside"><div slot="aside">…</div></Hero>
  //
  // The hero owns the BOX (`.hero-aside`, its bed, its column and its place in the grid) and
  // the typography of what goes in it, which is why the inner rules below are `:global()`
  // under `.hero-aside`: slotted content belongs to the caller's scope, not to this one.
  /** `layout: 'mosaic'` — the tiles the photo column becomes. Fewer than two falls back to
   *  the single photo, because a one-tile mosaic is a photo with extra markup.
   *
   *  `srcset`/`sizes` are per TILE and exist for `imageSrcset`'s reason, one size down: a
   *  tile is a cell of a two-column grid inside the media column, so it paints at roughly
   *  180px at 1280 and 140px at 375 — a public-path master decodes there at five to eight
   *  times the width it paints, which is `img-srcset-within-2x` (blocking) every time. The
   *  single-photo branch has been able to say what candidates a `/images/…` file has since
   *  the kit shipped; the mosaic could not, and this is the first page to mount one. Both
   *  are optional: a tile with no candidates renders exactly as it did before. */
  mosaic?: { src: string; alt: string; w?: number; h?: number; caption?: string;
             srcset?: string; sizes?: string }[];
};
// NOTHING IS ASSERTED HERE. The eyebrow, the title and the lede are the page's, and a hero
// mounted with none of them renders a photo and a CTA row — which is a hero with nothing to
// say, visibly, rather than a hero saying something nobody approved.
//
// The lede is held to two lines at the desktop clamp, which is what rule 10 leaves it: a
// third line pushes the CTA row past the 450px ceiling, so a page writing one keeps it short.
const {
  as: Heading = 'h1',
  eyebrow = '',
  title = '',
  lede = '',
  image,
  imageAlt,
  imageWidth,
  imageHeight,
  imageSrcset,
  imageSizes,
  chips = [],
  ctas = [],
  layout = 'split',
  align = 'left',
  media = 'right',
  ledge = 'none',
  ticks = [],
  stats = [],
  mosaic = [],
  class: cls,
  ...rest
} = Astro.props;
// A mosaic needs tiles. With fewer than two the layout renders as a split hero rather than
// as a grid with a hole in it — the arrangement degrades, it never invents a tile.
const tiles = layout === 'mosaic' && mosaic.length >= 2 ? mosaic.slice(0, 4) : [];
const asIsMosaic = tiles.length > 0;
// A ledge renders ONLY when the page handed it something. `none` is not a special case: an
// empty chip list and a `ledge: 'chips'` with no chips are the same hero.
// `chips` predates the ledge axis and three pages already pass it with no style behind them,
// so an UNSET ledge still shows them. A ledge that names a different block does not: a style
// whose ledge is `stats` is a style that put figures under the lede, and a hero showing both
// would be neither arrangement.
const showChips = chips.length > 0 && (ledge === 'chips' || ledge === 'none');
const showTicks = ledge === 'ticks' && ticks.length > 0;
const showStats = ledge === 'stats' && stats.length > 0;
const showAside = ledge === 'aside' && Astro.slots.has('aside');
// NOTHING IS ASSERTED HERE ANY MORE. The chip list and the CTA row were literals in this
// file; both are props now and both default to empty, so a hero states only what the page
// that mounted it handed it.

// ── WHAT THE HERO'S PHOTOGRAPHY ACTUALLY PAINTS ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `sizes` is a PROMISE about the box, and every value below was read off a real built page at
// twelve widths rather than reasoned about. The promise had been wrong on every arrangement at
// once: `(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 560px` is the SPLIT hero's desktop column, written before
// working rule 16 gave the hero five layouts, and measured at 1280 the split paints 408px
// (declared 560, over), stacked and bleed paint 828px (declared 560, UNDER — the browser was
// choosing a candidate too small for the box), and the panel with an aside paints 224px above
// 1200 and 144px between 1024 and 1199. Even the mobile branch was wrong by a third: `100vw`
// is the viewport and the photo is inside a padded container, so it paints 277px at 375.
//
// THE THREE BREAKPOINTS ARE THE CSS'S OWN, not round numbers: 900px is where every arrangement
// becomes one column (the `@media (max-width: 900px)` block at the foot of this file), 1024px
// is where `PageShell` gives the dial its column and the content band loses ~196px, and 1200px
// is where the container stops growing. A `sizes` list that ignored them would be right in one
// band and wrong in the next, which is how the single 560px reading survived this long.
//
// `img-sizes-matches-box` (tests/render/checks/img.ts) measures each of these against the box
// it paints at 375/768/1024/1280 on every built page and fails outside 0.9-1.25x, so a layout
// change that moves a column cannot leave a stale promise behind it again.
//
// A mosaic TILE is two columns inside the media column, so it is that column's width less the
// gap, halved. The aside arrangements narrow the media column and get their own list from the
// page (`mosaic[].sizes`); this is the default for a mosaic with no aside, measured on
// /buy-blue-staffy-puppies-uk/: 136px at 375, 332px at 768, 140px at 1024 and 182px at 1280.
const MOSAIC_SIZES = '(max-width: 900px) calc(50vw - 52px), '
  + '(max-width: 1023px) calc(23.8vw - 38px), (max-width: 1199px) calc(23.4vw - 100px), 182px';
// The single photo, per arrangement.
//   TOP    — stacked, bleed-on-top and panel-on-top are one column: the whole content band,
//            which is `100vw` less the container padding below 1024 and less the dial column
//            as well above it. Measured on /blue-staffy-pup-sale-uk/ and
//            /buy-staffy-puppies-for-sale-uk/: 279 / 672 / 652 / 828 at 375 / 768 / 1024 / 1280.
//   PANEL  — a `minmax(0, 14rem)` column, 224px, which the aside arrangement narrows to 9rem
//            between 1024 and 1199 (see the panel+aside block in the stylesheet). Measured on
//            /blue-staffy-health-uk/: 277 / 670 / 144 / 224.
//   SPLIT  — the two-column hero's media column, fluid between 900 and 1200 and capped at
//            408px. Measured on /: 277 / 670 / 315 / 408. This is also where a BLEED hero with
//            side media would land, and it is the one combination no approved record renders
//            today: its photo runs from its column to the viewport edge, so it declares `50vw`
//            from the CSS's own `margin-inline: calc(-50vw + 50%)` rather than from a
//            measurement, and `img-sizes-matches-box` is what will read it the day H-HM3 ships.
//   A MOSAIC WITH ONE PHOTO is the split hero, whatever its `media` says. `media: top` is
//            what STACKS a stacked, panel or bleed hero — each has its own one-column rule —
//            but no rule stacks a mosaic, so a mosaic that degraded to its single photo paints
//            the split hero's column. Reading `top` there promised 828px for a 408px box on
//            `/` while H-HM2's record named one photograph, which is the `sizes` defect this
//            block exists to prevent, one arrangement over. (Since Known Issue 33 / user ruling
//            R11 the homepage's record names four photographs and two figures, so `/` paints
//            the full mosaic; the rule still holds for any mosaic that degrades.)
const BELOW_900 = (px: number) => `(max-width: 900px) calc(100vw - ${px}px)`;
const stacksOnTop = media === 'top' && layout !== 'mosaic';
const MEASURED_PIC_SIZES = stacksOnTop
  ? `(max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 96px), (max-width: 1199px) calc(100vw - 372px), 828px`
  : layout === 'panel'
    ? (ledge === 'aside'
      ? `${BELOW_900(98)}, (max-width: 1023px) 224px, (max-width: 1199px) 144px, 224px`
      : `${BELOW_900(98)}, 224px`)
    : layout === 'bleed'
      ? `${BELOW_900(98)}, 50vw`
      : `${BELOW_900(98)}, (max-width: 1023px) calc(52.5vw - 78px), `
        + `(max-width: 1199px) calc(52.5vw - 224px), 408px`;
const PIC_SIZES = imageSizes ?? MEASURED_PIC_SIZES;
// NO DEFAULT ALT EITHER. The sentence that used to stand in here described the kit's own
// master, and it went with it: an alt is the page's description of the page's photograph.
const alt = imageAlt ?? '';
// A public path cannot go through <Image>: astro:assets only handles files it can read at
// build time out of src/. So a string src renders a plain <img> with the same class, sizes
// and loading behaviour, and the CSS below does not care which branch produced it.
const legacy = typeof image === 'string';
// A HERO WITH NO PHOTOGRAPH. `media: 'none'` is on the `Layout` axis and, until now, the
// component ignored it: every branch below painted a picture, so a page with nothing to show
// had to hand over somebody else's. That is the one thing working rules 9 and 11 both forbid
// — a blog post with no featured image on disk does not get a borrowed puppy to look
// illustrated — and `src/pages/[...post].astro` is the first caller that has no image to
// give. So the media column is DROPPED rather than filled: the copy takes the whole band,
// the rule 10 floor and ceiling still apply (`.inner` keeps its `min-height`, the section
// its `max-height`), and nothing else about the arrangement changes. No page passed
// `media="none"` before this, so no shipped hero moves.
const showPhoto = media !== 'none';
// A photo column with nothing to put in it, or a photo with nothing to say about it, stops the
// build with the caller's own heading in the message. A mosaic of two or more tiles carries its
// own per-tile alts and needs neither.
if (showPhoto && !(layout === 'mosaic' && mosaic.length >= 2)) {
  if (!image) {
    throw new Error(`Hero "${title}": no \`image\` and media is "${media}" — pass the page's own `
      + 'photograph, or media="none" for a hero that has none. There is no default photo.');
  }
  if (!alt) {
    throw new Error(`Hero "${title}": the photograph has no \`imageAlt\` — a page states what its `
      + 'own picture shows.');
  }
}
---
<section
  {...rest}
  class:list={['kit-hero', cls]}
  data-hero-layout={layout}
  data-hero-align={align}
  data-hero-media={media}
  data-hero-ledge={ledge}
>
  <div class="container inner">
    {!showPhoto ? null : asIsMosaic ? (
      /* Rule 10's DOM order holds for a mosaic too: the tiles PRECEDE the copy in source and
         `order` puts them back on the media side. The first tile is the LCP element and is
         the one that gets `eager`/`high`; the rest are lazy, because a hero that fetches four
         photos at high priority has no priority. */
      <ul class="mosaic pic">
        {tiles.map((t, i) => (
          <li>
            <img
              src={t.src}
              alt={t.alt}
              width={t.w}
              height={t.h}
              srcset={t.srcset}
              sizes={t.srcset ? (t.sizes ?? MOSAIC_SIZES) : undefined}
              loading={i === 0 ? 'eager' : 'lazy'}
              fetchpriority={i === 0 ? 'high' : 'auto'}
              decoding="async"
            />
            {t.caption && <span class="cap">{t.caption}</span>}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    ) : legacy ? (
      <img
        src={image as string}
        alt={alt}
        width={imageWidth}
        height={imageHeight}
        srcset={imageSrcset}
        sizes={PIC_SIZES}
        class="pic"
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
        decoding="async"
      />
    ) : (
      <Image
        src={image as ImageMetadata}
        alt={alt}
        widths={[480, 800, 1200]}
        sizes={PIC_SIZES}
        class="pic"
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
        decoding="async"
      />
    )}
    <div class="copy">
      {eyebrow && <p class="eyebrow">{eyebrow}</p>}
      {title && <Heading class="title">{title}</Heading>}
      {lede && <p class="lede">{lede}</p>}
      {showChips && (
        <ul class="chips">{chips.map((c) => <li class="kit-chip">{c}</li>)}</ul>
      )}
      {showTicks && (
        <ul class="ticks">
          {ticks.map((t) => (
            <li>
              <svg class="tick" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                   stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round"
                   stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"
              ><path d="M20 6 9 17l-5-5" /></svg>
              <span>{t}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
      {showStats && (
        <ul class="hstats">
          {stats.map((s) => <li><strong>{s.n}</strong><span>{s.label}</span></li>)}
        </ul>
      )}
      {ctas.length > 0 && (
        <div class="ctas">
          {ctas.map((c) => <Button kind={c.kind ?? 'primary'} href={c.href} label={c.label} />)}
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
    {showAside && (
      <aside class="hero-aside"><slot name="aside" /></aside>
    )}
  </div>
</section>
<style>
  @layer components {
    /* No `overflow: hidden` on the section. The clamp below crops the PHOTO; it is not a
       guillotine across the copy. Hiding the overflow here would let a hero whose CTA row
       no longer fits simply lose it, and still measure as a pass. */
    .kit-hero { background: var(--color-surface); color: var(--color-text); position: relative; }
    .inner { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.1fr 1fr; gap: var(--space-8); align-items: center; padding-block: var(--space-10); position: relative; }
    /* Rule 10's DOM order: the photo is first in source and second on screen. */
    .pic { order: 2; }
    .copy { order: 1; }
    /* Brass is 3.1:1 on bone, so the eyebrow here is steel, not brass. */
    .eyebrow { margin: 0 0 var(--space-3); font-size: var(--text-xs); font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-brand); }
    .title { margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 700; font-size: var(--text-4xl); line-height: var(--text-4xl--line-height); text-wrap: balance; color: var(--color-brand); }
    .lede { margin: 0 0 var(--space-5); font-size: var(--text-lg); max-width: 52ch; }
    .ctas { display: flex; gap: var(--space-3); flex-wrap: wrap; }
    .chips { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 var(--space-5); padding: 0; display: flex; gap: var(--space-2); flex-wrap: wrap; }
    /* .kit-chip (src/styles/kit.css) is the shape; the hero's chips sit on the bone
       surface rather than inside a card, so they take the raised fill and a hairline. */
    .chips li { padding: 6px 12px; background: var(--color-surface-raised); border: var(--card-border); }
    .pic { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; aspect-ratio: 4/5; object-fit: cover; border-radius: var(--card-radius); box-shadow: var(--shadow-lift); }
    /* RULE 10. A 390px floor so a short hero still reads as a hero, a 450px ceiling so it
       never eats the fold — and the COPY has to fit inside that ceiling on its own, because
       only the photo is allowed to be cropped by it. So: the ceiling sits on the SECTION
       (`max-height`), the floor on `.inner` (`min-height`, border-box, so the padding is
       inside the 390), the heading steps down to --text-3xl, the lede is held to two lines
       and the block margins tighten. The only `overflow: clip` is on the photo column: the
       picture drops its 4:5 crop, stretches to the row and is cropped by `object-fit`, so
       it can never set the height. */
    @media (min-width: 1024px) {
      .inner { min-height: 390px; padding-block: var(--space-6); }
      .eyebrow { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
      .title { font-size: var(--text-3xl); line-height: var(--text-3xl--line-height); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
      .lede { margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
      .chips { margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
      /* `height: 100%` alone is not enough: the grid row is sized BY its items, so the
         percentage has nothing definite to resolve against and the portrait master falls
         back to its intrinsic height — a 690px row that the ceiling then slices, taking the
         centred copy's CTA row with it. The cap is therefore absolute, and it is exactly
         the content box the 450px ceiling leaves once the padding is taken out. It stays on
         from 1024 even though the ceiling above it does not: the photo is what must never
         SET the height, at any width, and capping it is what makes the copy the only thing
         the band is sized by. */
      .pic { align-self: stretch; height: 100%; max-height: calc(450px - 2 * var(--space-6)); aspect-ratio: auto; object-fit: cover; overflow: clip; }
    }
    /* THE CEILING IS A DESKTOP MEASURE (spec §9 amendment 10.4 sub-note, breeder 2026-09-21).
       Rule 10's 390-450 band is measured at 1280 and the arrangements are budgeted for the
       copy column 1280 gives them. Between 1024 and 1279 that column is narrower and the same
       words take more lines, so a ceiling written for 1280 stops being a budget and becomes a
       blade: it hid three to six lines of four pages' own opening sentences behind the lede's
       two-line clamp, and let the homepage's and the listing's hero content run 17px and 21px
       into the section below (Known Issue 28, measured). A VERBATIM H1 WINS BELOW 1280 —
       working rule 15 puts the old H1 on the page word for word, so it cannot be shortened to
       fit a band, and the band is what gives way. The clamp and the lede's line-clamp are
       therefore scoped to 1280 and up; the 390 floor, the type step-down and the photo's own
       cap stay on from 1024, so the band still reads as a hero and still cannot be set by its
       photograph. Below 1024 neither has ever applied. */
    @media (min-width: 1280px) {
      .kit-hero { max-height: 450px; }
      .lede { display: -webkit-box; -webkit-box-orient: vertical; -webkit-line-clamp: 2; line-clamp: 2; overflow: hidden; }
    }
    @media (max-width: 900px) {
      .inner { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
      .title { font-size: var(--text-3xl); }
      .pic { aspect-ratio: 3/2; }
    }

    /* `media: 'none'` — no photo, so no media column. One column at every width, and the
       copy is allowed the full measure rather than the 52ch a two-column hero holds it to,
       because there is no picture beside it for the line length to relate to. The rule 10
       floor and ceiling are inherited untouched: a photoless hero is still a hero band. */
    .kit-hero[data-hero-media="none"] .inner { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .kit-hero[data-hero-media="none"] .lede { max-width: 68ch; }

    /* ── working rule 16: the per-page arrangements ──────────────────────────────────────
       Everything above is the SPLIT hero, which is what `data-hero-layout="split"` still
       gets and what the four pages built before this rule still render. The blocks below are
       the other four arrangements and the four ledges, and every one of them is held to the
       same rule 10 ceiling: the band is 390-450px at 1024px and up, the copy is SIZED to fit
       and only the photo is cropped. A layout that cannot make its copy fit does not get to
       hide the overflow — it gets a shorter photo. */

    /* The ledges. All four sit in the copy column, under the lede, above the CTA row. */
    .ticks { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); padding: 0;
             display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-2) var(--space-5); }
    .ticks li { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--space-2); font-size: var(--text-sm); }
    .tick { color: var(--color-cta); flex: none; }
    .hstats { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 var(--space-4); padding: 0;
              display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--space-5); align-items: baseline; }
    .hstats li { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 2px; }
    .hstats strong { font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: var(--text-xl); line-height: 1.1;
                     font-feature-settings: "tnum"; color: var(--color-brand); }
    .hstats span { font-size: var(--text-xs); color: var(--color-text); }
    /* The aside is a THIRD column, not a card floated into the copy: the copy has to keep
       its measure or the lede's two-line clamp starts hiding words.
       IT PAINTS ITS OWN LIGHT BED, SO IT OWNS ITS OWN INK. `--color-surface-raised` is a
       light fill, and on a hero inside `.bl-frame-band` the inherited colour is
       `--color-text-on-inverse` — bone text on a near-white card, measured at 1.05:1, which
       is a quote nobody can read and a defect no gate could see because every OTHER element
       in the box (`.aside-title`, `.aside-rows dt`) happened to name a colour of its own.
       Anything that paints a surface states the ink for it (global.css conventions 2-3), so
       the box does it once here for everything slotted into it.
       `tests/render/meta.spec.ts` measures the contrast of `.aside-quote` against its own
       painted background, and the fixture pair `kit-hero-aside-*.html` pins both readings. */
    .hero-aside { order: 3; align-self: center; background: var(--color-surface-raised);
                  color: var(--color-text);
                  border: var(--card-border); border-radius: var(--card-radius);
                  padding: var(--space-4); font-size: var(--text-sm); }
    .hero-aside :global(.aside-title) { margin: 0 0 var(--space-2); font-size: var(--text-xs); font-weight: 700;
                   letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-brand); }
    .hero-aside :global(.aside-quote) { margin: 0; font-family: var(--font-display); font-size: var(--text-lg);
                   line-height: 1.3; border-inline-start: 3px solid var(--color-cta);
                   padding-inline-start: var(--space-3); }
    /* A credential line under the quote — the same content a `chips` ledge would put under the
       lede, folded onto one line because an aside is a column and a wrapped chip row costs a
       band it cannot spare. The hero owns the typography of what is slotted into it, which is
       why this lives here with the rest of the `.aside-*` set. */
    .hero-aside :global(.aside-chips) { margin: var(--space-3) 0 0; font-size: var(--text-xs);
                   font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-text-muted); }
    .hero-aside :global(.aside-rows) { margin: 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
                  gap: var(--space-1) var(--space-3); }
    .hero-aside :global(.aside-rows dt) { color: var(--color-text-muted); }
    .hero-aside :global(.aside-rows dd) { margin: 0; font-weight: 600; }
    .hero-aside :global(.aside-items) { margin: 0; padding-inline-start: var(--space-4); display: grid; gap: var(--space-1); }
    /* THE SEAM BETWEEN THE FACTS AND THE CREDENTIAL LINE. An aside that carries a list of facts
       AND the credential line under it needs the two told apart, and both pages that mount this
       arrangement had written the same rule in their own scope — /blue-staffy-health-uk/ for its
       `dl` and /uk-staffordshire-bull-terrier-guide/ for its `ul`. A rule copied into two pages
       is a rule that can be fixed in one, so it lives here with the rest of the aside's
       typography, which the hero already owns. */
    .hero-aside :global(:is(.aside-rows, .aside-items) + .aside-chips) {
      border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border); padding-top: var(--space-2);
    }
    /* A CONTENTS LIST IN A PANEL'S ASIDE IS SIZED FOR THE CEILING, and that is a measurement:
       at the kit's default size three contents lines plus a title plus a credential row made
       the breed guide's aside 180px and pushed the band 19px past 450 at 1024 and 30px past it
       at 1280 — clipped, which is a defect a screenshot does not show. `--text-xs` with the gap
       closed is 34px and is the whole of the difference. Scoped to `panel` + `aside`, which is
       the arrangement with the narrowest aside column and the tightest budget, and to
       `.aside-items` only: a `dl` of key facts is three short rows and is left alone. */
    .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] :global(.aside-items) {
      font-size: var(--text-xs); line-height: 1.35; gap: 0;
    }
    .kit-hero[data-hero-ledge="aside"] .inner { grid-template-columns: 1.1fr 1fr; }
    @media (min-width: 1024px) {
      .kit-hero[data-hero-ledge="aside"] .inner { grid-template-columns: 1fr minmax(0, 1fr) 15rem; }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-ledge="aside"][data-hero-layout="panel"] .inner {
        grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 14rem) minmax(0, 1fr) 15rem;
      }
    }

    /* Centre alignment. The lede loses its 52ch cap or a centred column reads as ragged. */
    .kit-hero[data-hero-align="center"] .copy { text-align: center; }
    .kit-hero[data-hero-align="center"] .lede { max-width: 60ch; margin-inline: auto; }
    .kit-hero[data-hero-align="center"] .ctas,
    .kit-hero[data-hero-align="center"] .chips,
    .kit-hero[data-hero-align="center"] .ticks,
    .kit-hero[data-hero-align="center"] .hstats { justify-content: center; }

    /* MOSAIC — the photo column becomes up to four tiles. The column geometry is the split
       hero's, so the clamp behaves identically; only what fills the column changes. */
    .mosaic { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: var(--space-2);
              grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; box-shadow: none; }
    .mosaic li { position: relative; margin: 0; }
    .mosaic img { display: block; width: 100%; height: 100%; aspect-ratio: 1;
                  object-fit: cover; border-radius: var(--card-radius); }
    /* The caption sits ON the tile, as the idea sheets draw it, so it reads as a label for
       that photo rather than as a sentence of the page. Steel scrim, bone text. */
    .mosaic .cap { position: absolute; inset-inline: var(--space-2); bottom: var(--space-2);
                   background: var(--color-surface-inverse); color: var(--color-text-on-inverse);
                   font-size: var(--text-xs); padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: var(--card-radius); }
    @media (min-width: 1024px) {
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="mosaic"] .mosaic {
        max-height: calc(450px - 2 * var(--space-6)); overflow: clip;
      }
    }

    /* MOSAIC WITH AN ASIDE is the only arrangement in the eighteen that puts THREE columns
       beside each other — tiles, copy, aside — so the `1fr minmax(0, 1fr) 15rem` the aside
       ledge sets leaves it the narrowest copy column on the site: 246px at 1280 and 158px at
       1024, measured on /blue-staffy-uk-breeders/, where the approved H1 took seven lines in
       it and ran 41px of copy past the 450px ceiling while the two-line lede clamp quietly
       hid a third and a fourth line. H-AB1 is the one style in the eighteen with this pair.
       THREE COLUMNS DO NOT FIT AT ANY DESKTOP WIDTH: the band is ~800px of content at 1280
       and ~620px at 1024, and three tracks plus two gaps leave the copy under 250px of it
       however they are split — every split measured ran either the H1 or the clamped lede
       over. So the aside becomes a SECOND ROW beneath the copy, beside the tiles, and the
       arrangement stays the one the breeder picked: tiles left, copy right, the quote in its
       own box. The other two changes are `panel`'s answer to the same problem (see below) —
       the TILES give up their equal share, which costs them nothing because they are
       height-capped above and crop either way, and the TYPE STEPS DOWN. Measured on that
       page with the aside carrying a quote and a credential line: 433px at 1024, 390px at
       1100, 448px at 1280, hero overflow and lede overflow 0 at all three. */
    @media (min-width: 1024px) {
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="mosaic"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .inner {
        grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 16rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
        grid-template-areas: "pic copy" "pic aside";
        align-items: start;
      }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="mosaic"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .pic { grid-area: pic; }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="mosaic"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .copy { grid-area: copy; }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="mosaic"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .hero-aside {
        grid-area: aside; align-self: start;
      }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="mosaic"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .title {
        font-size: var(--text-2xl); line-height: var(--text-2xl--line-height);
        margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
      }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="mosaic"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .lede {
        margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
      }
    }
    /* Between 1024 and 1199 the band is ~620px of content rather than ~800 and the copy has
       180px less to spend, so everything in the column takes its share of the difference: the
       tiles narrow again, the aside's box and its gaps tighten, and the QUOTE steps out of
       display type — at 1280 it is the aside's one piece of display type and it fits, at 1024
       the same sentence was the difference between 448px and 502px. Measured one rule at a
       time: 161px over the ceiling with the aside first filled, then 99, 52, 15, 7 and 0.
       1280 is outside the query and is unaffected by any of it. */
    @media (min-width: 1024px) and (max-width: 1199px) {
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="mosaic"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .inner {
        grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 9rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
      }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="mosaic"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .hero-aside {
        padding: var(--space-3);
      }
      /* The quote steps down with everything else here. At 1280 it is the aside's one piece of
         display type and it fits; at 1024 the band has 180px less to spend and the same
         sentence is the difference between 448px and 502px, measured. */
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="mosaic"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] :global(.aside-quote) {
        font-size: var(--text-base);
      }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="mosaic"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .lede {
        margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
      }
      /* 1024 exactly is the narrowest desktop and the last 15px: the aside's own gaps, which
         are display-type spacing it no longer has display type to space. */
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="mosaic"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] :global(.aside-chips) {
        margin-top: var(--space-2);
      }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="mosaic"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] :global(.aside-title) {
        margin-bottom: var(--space-1);
      }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="mosaic"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .inner {
        gap: var(--space-5);
      }
    }

    /* PANEL — text-led. The photo keeps rule 10's DOM position but takes a narrow column, so
       the page reads as prose with a picture beside it rather than as a sales band. The copy
       column is WIDER than a split hero's, which is exactly why its type steps down: measured,
       the blog family's H-BL3 ran 23px of copy past the ceiling on a long H1 before it did. */
    .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"] .inner { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 14rem) minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"] .pic { aspect-ratio: 3/4; }
    @media (min-width: 1024px) {
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"] .title {
        font-size: var(--text-2xl); line-height: var(--text-2xl--line-height); margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
      }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"] .lede { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"] .chips,
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"] .ticks,
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"] .hstats { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
    }
    /* A STATS ledge is the tallest of the four — two lines per figure — and a stacked hero has
       already spent 150px of its budget on the photo. Measured: the why-us page's H-FS3 ran
       8px over before the figures were put on one line here. */
    @media (min-width: 1024px) {
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="stacked"] .hstats li { flex-direction: row; align-items: baseline; gap: var(--space-2); }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="stacked"] .hstats strong { font-size: var(--text-lg); }
    }

    /* BLEED — the photo spills to the edge of the band on its own side and drops its radius
       and lift; it is the surface there, not an object on it. */
    .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="bleed"] .pic { border-radius: 0; box-shadow: none; }
    @media (min-width: 1024px) {
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="bleed"] .inner { padding-block: 0; }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="bleed"] .copy { padding-block: var(--space-6); }
      /* 450 is a FLOOR here and a ceiling only where the ceiling still applies. Below 1280
         the band grows to the copy (the sub-note above), and a bleed photo pinned to 450 in a
         480px band would leave a strip of bare bed at the edge it is supposed to BE — so it
         stretches to the row and 450 becomes the minimum rather than the height. */
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="bleed"]:not([data-hero-media="top"]) .pic {
        align-self: stretch; height: auto; min-height: 450px; margin-inline-end: calc(-50vw + 50%);
      }
      /* `media: top` is a bleed hero STACKED — a slim photo band over the copy, on the same
         150px budget as `panel`+top and `stacked`. `min-height` beats `max-height` in the
         cascade, so the stretch rule above must not reach it: unscoped it made the why-us
         hero's 150px strip 450px tall and ran the band 312px past its own box. */
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="bleed"][data-hero-media="top"] .pic {
        margin-inline-end: calc(-50vw + 50%);
      }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="bleed"][data-hero-media="left"] .pic {
        margin-inline: calc(-50vw + 50%) 0;
      }
    }

    /* STACKED — photo ABOVE the copy. The ceiling stays on (a stacked hero that released it
       would be the one page on the site with a 700px band), so the photo is a short landscape
       strip and the copy is what the remaining space is budgeted for. Measured, not asserted:
       npm run test:render:pages reads the band height at 1280 and the CTA row's bottom edge. */
    /* The photo is already FIRST in source order (rule 10); a stacked hero is the one
       arrangement that wants it first on screen too, so the `order` the split hero uses to
       send it right has to be undone rather than inherited. */
    .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="stacked"] .inner { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="stacked"] .pic { order: 1; aspect-ratio: 24/5; }
    .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="stacked"] .copy { order: 2; }
    .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="stacked"] .hero-aside { order: 3; }
    @media (min-width: 1024px) {
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="stacked"] .inner { gap: var(--space-4); padding-block: var(--space-5); }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="stacked"] .pic { height: 150px; max-height: 150px; aspect-ratio: auto; }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="stacked"] .title { font-size: var(--text-2xl); margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="stacked"] .lede { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="stacked"] .chips,
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="stacked"] .ticks,
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="stacked"] .hstats { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
    }
    /* A STACKED hero with an aside puts the aside BESIDE the copy, not under it. Measured:
       as a third row at 1280 it took the blog's H-BL2 13px past the ceiling, which is a hero
       that fits only because something is scrolled out of sight. */
    @media (min-width: 1024px) {
      /* Explicit areas rather than `order`: a stacked hero with an aside is a photo across
         the top and two columns under it, and auto-placement plus three `order` values put
         the aside on a third row instead — 13px past the ceiling, measured on the blog
         family's H-BL2 before this block existed. */
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="stacked"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .inner {
        grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 15rem;
        grid-template-areas: "pic pic" "copy aside";
      }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="stacked"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .pic { grid-area: pic; }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="stacked"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .copy { grid-area: copy; }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="stacked"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .hero-aside {
        grid-area: aside; align-self: start;
      }
    }

    /* A SPLIT HERO WITH AN ASIDE is the same problem a third time. H-GD1, the interior-guide
       family's editorial split, is the one style with this pair, and no page had rendered it
       until the three guides were re-boarded (Known Issues 30 and 35, project 5 readiness).
       `ledge: 'aside'` sets three tracks above 1024, which left the copy 276px at 1280:
       measured, the breed guide's verbatim H1 took eight lines in it and the buying guide's
       ten. So the aside becomes a SECOND ROW beneath the copy and the photo keeps its side at
       the full height of both rows — the arrangement the style names, with the key facts in
       their own box. Even so it holds only a short H1: with the faces loaded it fits the health
       guide, whose page leaves its credential line out of this arrangement, and runs the breed
       guide's hero 131px past 450. */
    @media (min-width: 1024px) {
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="split"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .inner {
        grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
        grid-template-areas: "copy pic" "aside pic";
        align-items: start; row-gap: var(--space-3);
      }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="split"][data-hero-ledge="aside"][data-hero-media="left"] .inner {
        grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
        grid-template-areas: "pic copy" "pic aside";
      }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="split"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .pic { grid-area: pic; }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="split"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .copy { grid-area: copy; }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="split"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .hero-aside {
        grid-area: aside; align-self: start; padding: var(--space-3);
      }
      /* Two rows in one column is the mosaic's budget, so it takes the mosaic's type step:
         the title one notch down and the lede's margin tightened (measured on
         /blue-staffy-health-uk/). */
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="split"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .title {
        font-size: var(--text-2xl); line-height: var(--text-2xl--line-height);
        margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
      }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="split"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .lede { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
    }

    /* A PANEL WITH AN ASIDE IS THE MOSAIC'S PROBLEM AGAIN, and it takes the mosaic's answer.
       `ledge: 'aside'` sets `1fr minmax(0, 1fr) 15rem` above 1024 and the panel narrows the
       first track to 14rem, which still leaves THREE tracks and two gaps in ~620px of content
       at 1024: measured on /blue-staffy-health-uk/ (H-GD3), the copy column was under 150px,
       the H1 ran 211px of section overflow past the ceiling and the clamped two-line lede hid
       297px of itself — invisible on the page, because a clamp does not push, it cuts. So the
       aside becomes a SECOND ROW beneath the copy, beside the photo, exactly as it does on
       H-AB1, and the arrangement stays the one the breeder picked: photo left, copy right, the
       key facts in their own box. Measured after, with the aside carrying three rows and a
       credential line: no section overflow and no lede overflow at 1024, 1100 or 1280. */
    @media (min-width: 1024px) {
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .inner {
        grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 14rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
        grid-template-areas: "pic copy" "pic aside";
        align-items: start;
      }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .pic { grid-area: pic; }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .copy { grid-area: copy; }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .hero-aside {
        grid-area: aside; align-self: start; padding: var(--space-3);
      }
      /* The ROW gap is the one the two-row grid spends twice and needs once: at 1280 the
         column gap is 48px and the same value between the copy and the aside was 29 of the
         30px the band ran over, measured. The column gap is untouched. */
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .inner { row-gap: var(--space-3); }
    }
    /* Between 1024 and 1199 the band is ~620px of content rather than ~800, so the photo column
       narrows and the aside's own spacing tightens — the same order the mosaic's fix was
       measured in, and for the same reason: the copy column is what the difference comes out
       of otherwise. 1280 is outside the query and is unaffected. */
    @media (min-width: 1024px) and (max-width: 1199px) {
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .inner {
        grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 9rem) minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--space-5);
      }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .lede { margin-bottom: var(--space-2); }
      /* The copy column is ~180px narrower here, so a long H1 takes a third line: 108px of
         title against 72px at 1280, which is the last 21px of the ceiling. The type steps down
         one notch rather than the aside losing a row of facts. */
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] .title {
        font-size: var(--text-xl); line-height: var(--text-xl--line-height);
      }
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"][data-hero-ledge="aside"] :global(.aside-title) {
        margin-bottom: var(--space-1);
      }
    }

    /* A PANEL that puts its photo on top is one column, not a narrow photo column beside the
       copy — a title panel with a slim photo band over it. Same height budget as `stacked`,
       for the same reason: the ceiling stays on, so the photo is short and the copy is what
       the rest is spent on. */
    .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"][data-hero-media="top"] .inner { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"][data-hero-media="top"] .pic { order: 1; aspect-ratio: 24/5; }
    .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"][data-hero-media="top"] .copy { order: 2; }
    @media (min-width: 1024px) {
      .kit-hero[data-hero-layout="panel"][data-hero-media="top"] .inner {
        gap: var(--space-4); padding-block: var(--space-5);
      }
